### FEATURE_DISCOVERY — the crossing: the one thing that moves between the two zones, and the two refusals
###
### Every request here is a real one against a running instance. Sign in first: the session cookie and
### the cross-site token are what the chain expects. The demo backend runs on 8081 — Tomcat9 owns 8080
### on this machine.
###
### **DECISION-DISCOVERY-ZONE-01 draws two zones** — Discovery *observes*, the application *executes*,
### they share no tables — and exactly one thing crosses between them in exactly one direction: **an
### approved template.** Everything in `discovery.http`, `discovery-tracks.http` and
### `discovery-canvas.http` writes rows this zone owns and can afford to be wrong about; the requests
### here write into a governed library that running work references.
###
### **There are three such requests and this file holds two of them.** The third —
### DISCOVERY-WRITE-IT-DOWN-01, a recurring shape authored as a process — is in
### `discovery-analysis.http` with the rest of the pipeline page's calls. Its code sits in
### `FormalisationController` beside these two, because what decides where a door lives is what it
### writes; its walkthrough sits with the page it is pressed from, because what decides where a
### walkthrough lives is who is reading it. Said here so the count is not read as two.
###
### **What to watch while demonstrating this, and it is a count rather than a screen.** Before the first
### request and after the last, run:
###
###   select (select count(*) from task) as tasks, (select count(*) from process_instance) as runs;
###
### The two numbers are identical afterwards. A node became a task *template* and a thread became a
### process *template*; nobody was given any work, and no run started. Describing how work is done and
### deciding to do it are different acts belonging to different zones, and only the first happens here.
###
### Seed the workspace in discovery mode, then use `discovery.http` to open an engagement and mark two
### requests in one thread. There is nothing to formalise until something has been marked:
###   ./mvnw spring-boot:run -Dspring-boot.run.arguments="--discovery"

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@manager = ionut@atelier.ro
@employee = andrei@atelier.ro
@password = a-long-enough-passphrase

### Copy real identifiers out of `discovery.http`'s responses into these. `nodeId` and `secondNodeId`
### must be two units of work in the SAME thread — the same pair in the same engagement — or the
### composition below has nothing to gate on.
@nodeId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@secondNodeId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@trackId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Copy the `taskTemplateId` out of the first response into this, to read it back through TASKLIB.
@taskTemplateId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Copy the `processTemplateId` out of the composition response into this.
@processTemplateId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Sign in as the owner. She holds WORK_NODE_MARK, TASK_TEMPLATE_CREATE, TASK_TEMPLATE_APPROVE and
### PROCESS_TEMPLATE_AUTHOR — which is what the two endpoints below need between them.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{owner}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###############################################################################
### DISCOVERY-FORMALISE-WORK-01 — a node becomes a template, and never a task
###############################################################################

### 201. The crossing point. Requires WORK_NODE_MARK plus TASKLIB's own TASK_TEMPLATE_CREATE and
### TASK_TEMPLATE_APPROVE — **called rather than copied**, and re-checked by TASKLIB on its own side of
### the wall. No permission in DISCOVERY creates a template; the one thing that crosses does so under the
### receiving zone's authority (DISCOVERY_02 §4).
###
### **There is no auto-create path in this feature and there is no confidence high enough to add one**
### (invariant I7). A template that appeared without a person floods a shared vocabulary with words
### nobody chose, which is the failure this design is built against. Note what the request body cannot
### say: there is no `confidence` field, no `auto` field, and no shape a background pass could fill in.
### The form is pre-filled from what was observed so that agreeing costs almost nothing; **agreeing is
### still what happens.**
###
### `steps` become the template's checklist (DISCOVERY_03 §3) and are optional — a node nobody broke down
### is ordinary, and inventing steps would be the product writing somebody's checklist for them.
###
### The node is **not consumed**. Read `work_node` afterwards: the row is still there, still says what it
### said, and has gained a `task_template_id`. That column has no foreign key, deliberately — Discovery
### records that the crossing happened and does not own TASKLIB's table.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}/formalise
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Moodboard for a rebrand",
  "detail": "The first visual pass, before any logo work starts.",
  "steps": [
    "Collect references",
    "Lay out the board",
    "Send it for a first reaction"
  ]
}

### 200. **The same template, read back through TASKLIB's own door.** Worth doing in a demonstration
### rather than trusting the response above: an identifier in a response proves a UUID was generated,
### not that a row exists in another feature's table under that feature's rules.
###
### It comes back APPROVED — a person saved it with the evidence in front of them, which is better
### curation than a queue somebody works through a week later — and it carries the three steps as its
### `checklist`. It has an author, and the author is the person who pressed save: a template nobody wrote
### is one nobody can be asked about.
GET {{host}}/api/task-templates/{{taskTemplateId}}

### The second unit of work in the same thread, so the composition below has two steps rather than one.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{secondNodeId}}/formalise
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Logo variants",
  "detail": null,
  "steps": []
}

### 409 NODE_ALREADY_TEMPLATED. **Send the first request twice and this is the second answer.**
###
### Two templates from one observation is two vocabulary entries for one activity, and repointing the
### node to the second would silently disown the first — which a process template may already reference.
### It is refused **before** anything is created, so the library gains nothing: a refusal that had
### already written the row it then refused to record would be the duplicate it exists to prevent. Count
### `task_template` either side of this request and the number is unchanged.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}/formalise
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Moodboard, second attempt",
  "detail": null,
  "steps": []
}

### 422 UNKNOWN_WORK_NODE. A unit of work that does not exist is refused rather than conjured. One
### created on demand here would be an observation with nothing behind it, and would then be formalised
### into a library entry describing work nobody did.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111/formalise
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Work nobody observed",
  "detail": null,
  "steps": []
}

###############################################################################
### DISCOVERY-COMPOSE-PROCESS-01 — the vocabulary before the grammar
###############################################################################

### 409 TRACK_NOT_FULLY_TEMPLATED. **Send this BEFORE formalising the second node and this is the
### answer** — which is why it sits here rather than at the end of the file.
###
### **Rule 2 made mechanical.** A process template contains only task templates (invariant I6), so a
### thread holding one unit of work nobody has formalised has no step to offer for it. The alternative is
### a placeholder step named after whatever sentence somebody sent, which is how an SOP ships with a hole
### in it — and a hole in an SOP reads to whoever follows it as a step nobody bothered to write down
### rather than as one still being written.
###
### **The vocabulary must exist before the grammar.** Templating each node first is not an obstacle
### placed in front of composing a process; it is the composing, done in the order that makes the result
### mean anything.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/tracks/{{trackId}}/compose
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rebrand"
}

### 201. Every unit of work in the thread is in the library, so the thread becomes a process template.
### Requires WORK_NODE_MARK plus PROCESS's own PROCESS_TEMPLATE_AUTHOR — what ProcessTemplateController
### demands to author a template by hand. A caller who may not write a process template on that screen
### may not write one through this door either.
###
### **The request cannot name the steps, and that absence is deliberate.** They are the thread's own
### units of work, oldest first, which is the only order an observation has. A caller that could name the
### steps could name a step the thread does not contain, and the process a person got would be a
### different process from the one they were looking at.
###
### **This is the product's actual claim.** A process template now exists describing how this business
### rebrands, derived from two sentences two people sent each other in a chat — nobody wrote it, nobody
### filled in a form describing the business, and the workspace was seeded with no library at all.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/tracks/{{trackId}}/compose
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rebrand"
}

### 200. The composed template through PROCESS's own door, with its whole graph.
###
### Two steps, each naming a `taskTemplateId` and none carrying free text — invariant I6, visible in the
### response shape rather than asserted in prose. `expectedDurationHours` is null on both: the zone
### measures observed work-phase durations and deliberately does not hand one over here, because a
### template's expected duration is what somebody planning the work believes and an observed median is
### what happened. Presenting the second as the first would put a number in the library that the very
### evidence it came from has already contradicted.
###
### `overview` is null for the same reason. A thread carries no prose about itself — it is a sequence of
### things that happened — and writing one here would put a sentence in the process library that nobody
### in the workspace typed.
GET {{host}}/api/process-templates/{{processTemplateId}}

###############################################################################
### The permission split, on the door it protects
###############################################################################

### Sign in as Andrei, who marks work all day.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{employee}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 403 NOT_PERMITTED. WORK_NODE_MARK is the one permission in this product deliberately granted to
### everybody, because **nothing exists to analyse unless the people doing the work click** — it is not
### a licence to author a process template. That is PROCESS_TEMPLATE_AUTHOR, PROCESS's own, held by the
### owner and managers. Refused before the thread is even looked for.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/tracks/{{trackId}}/compose
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Rebrand"
}

### 403 NOT_PERMITTED, and this one is worth a second look because Andrei DOES hold TASK_TEMPLATE_CREATE
### — V45 grants it to every role precisely so that anybody may propose a template. He does not hold
### TASK_TEMPLATE_APPROVE, and this endpoint creates an *approved* entry, so both are required. The
### refusal comes from TASKLIB's own published surface rather than from anything DISCOVERY decided.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/nodes/{{nodeId}}/formalise
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Something Andrei observed",
  "detail": null,
  "steps": []
}
